The sum of the squares of the roots of a quadratic of the form $ax^2+bx+c$ is equal to $\left(\frac{-b}{a}\right)^2-\frac{2c}{a}$.
In this case, the sum of the squares of the roots is $\left(\frac{-12}{8}\right)^2-\frac{2(-14)}{8}=\left(\frac{-3}{2}\right)^2+\frac{7}{2}=\frac{9}{4}+\frac{7}{2}=\frac{9+14}{4}=\frac{23}{4}$, which is already in lowest terms.
Thus, the fraction that represents the sum of the squares of the roots is $\boxed{\frac{23}{4}}$.The answer is: \frac{23}{4}

We can find the remainder when each term of the Lucas sequence is divided by 8 and look for a pattern.
\begin{align*} 1 &\equiv 1 \pmod{8} \\ 3 &\equiv 3 \pmod{8} \\ 4 &\equiv 4 \pmod{8} \\ 7 &\equiv 7 \pmod{8} \\ 11 &\equiv 3 \pmod{8} \\ 18 &\equiv 2 \pmod{8} \\ 29 &\equiv 5 \pmod{8} \\ 47 &\equiv 7 \pmod{8} \\ 76 &\equiv 4 \pmod{8} \\ 123 &\equiv 3 \pmod{8} \\ \end{align*}
We can see that the sequence repeats every 6 terms. Therefore, the remainder when the $100^\text{th}$ term is divided by 8 is the same as the remainder when the $100\bmod6=4^\text{th}$ term is divided by 8. The fourth term is 7, so the remainder is $\boxed{7}$.The answer is: 7

